Context: the opening section of a manuscript on Gauss–Manin connections, Picard–Fuchs equations and the Cartier operation, about [1972]. Grothendieck is assembling the dictionary of 'equivalent data' for forms of PGL(2) over a scheme X, so that later he can put connections on them. Page 85 did this without extra structure: a P^1-bundle P, a rank-2 bundle E modulo twists, a rank-4 Azumaya algebra M, the Lie algebras g and g', and the groups G and G'. The top of this page records that E is the module realising M, g, g', G and G', with g = Lie G and g' = Lie G'.

Section 1.7 refines the dictionary by adding a section, i.e. a Borel reduction. The following data are equivalent:
(a) a P^1-bundle f: P → X with a section σ;
(b) an extension 0 → Ω → ℒ → O_X → 0 with Ω invertible;
(c) an Azumaya algebra M with a distinguished one-sided ideal (the annihilator of Ω);
(d, d') g (resp. g') with a Borel subalgebra ℒ (resp. ℒ');
(e, e') G (resp. G') with a Borel subgroup B (resp. B').

Passing from (a) to (b): take an invertible sheaf L on P of relative degree 1, rigidified by σ*L ≅ O_X. Then ℒ = f_*L. Because a degree-1 section vanishing to order 2 along σ is zero, ℒ ≅ f_*(L/J^2 L) ≅ σ*P^1_{P/X}(L), where J is the ideal of σ(X) and P^1 denotes first-order principal parts. This makes Ω the conormal sheaf J/J^2 of σ(X) in P. Conversely P = P(ℒ), and σ is the invertible quotient ℒ → O_X. The complement P − σ(X) is then an affine-line bundle, a torsor under a line bundle, whose class is the extension class in H^1(X, Ω) = Ext^1(O_X, Ω).

Algebra side: M = End ℒ. Since det ℒ ≅ Ω, we get g = M/O_X ≅ Sym^2 ℒ ⊗ Ω^{-1}. The decreasing filtration of Sym^2 ℒ has graded pieces Ω^2, Ω, O_X. After twisting by Ω^{-1} the pieces become Ω, O_X, Ω^{-1}, which are the root-space grading of sl2. The middle step is Fil_2 = Ω^{-1} ⊗ Ker(Sym^2 ℒ → Sym^2 O_X) = Ω^{-1} ⊗ Ω·ℒ ≅ ℒ. Hence Ω ⊂ ℒ ⊂ g: the Borel subalgebra is canonically the extension ℒ itself, with nilradical Ω and Cartan quotient O_X. Killing duality gives Ω ⊥ ℒ and g/Ω ≅ ℒ^∨ ≅ ℒ ⊗ Ω^{-1}. On the group side, G' = SL(ℒ) with B' the stabiliser of Ω, and (G, B) is obtained by dividing out the centre μ_2.

Section 1.8 is the purpose of all this. For X/S, consider triples (P, σ, ∇) with ∇ a connection on P relative to S. These are to be described via the extension (ℒ, Ω) plus a connection on g. Combined with a Kodaira–Spencer/transversality condition (item a may already read 'section transversale'), such a triple is a PGL2-oper / indigenous bundle / projective structure. This is the natural object behind second-order Picard–Fuchs equations, and in characteristic p it interacts with p-curvature and the Cartier operator. The next page treats the split case (two disjoint sections, g = Ω ⊕ O ⊕ Ω^{-1}).
